BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2457996-heim-colloquium-who-owns-translations-on-literary-ri"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:Heim Colloquium: "Who owns translations? On Literary Rights and Cu
 ltural Ownership"
DTSTART:20261107T143000Z
DTEND:20261107T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20261103T094828Z
UID:20261107T143000Z-2471932@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261107T143000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/429/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,768,897/23500_michaelhenryheim.rev.1727113370.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:The Heim Colloquium is made possible by a generous donation to
  Indiana University by the family of Michael Henry Heim (1943 – 2012)\, 
 one of the most eminent literary translators of the late twentieth- and ea
 rly twenty-first century in the US. The Heim bequest allows Indiana Univer
 sity to organize events on themes relevant to the many facets of Michael H
 enry Heim's work. For our inaugural colloquia\, we chose to focus on the i
 magined reader of literary translations (in 2024) and translator paratexts
 \, the introductions and afterwords translators have written about their w
 ork (in 2025). For this year's event\, we invited a core group of translat
 ion-focused graduate students at IU Bloomington to take the lead in formin
 g the questions to be addressed and inviting a panel of experts to help us
  address them.  The title of this year's colloquium is "Who Owns Translati
 on?"  Who owns translations? Is it the translator? The author of the sourc
 e text? The publisher? The "foundation" and other estates that control the
  rights? Our colloquium explores the question of ownership from theoretica
 l\, practical\, and speculative perspectives. First\, what are some ways t
 hat the translator conceptually "owns" the translation? At issue are quest
 ions of how translators make choices that redistribute narrative authority
 \, how they develop a responsibility for the work of another\, and whether
  certain texts "invite" co-ownership while others do not.  We would also l
 ike to explore how the literary market shapes questions of ownership. How 
 does the contemporary culture of self-promotion and social media play a ro
 le in this ownership? What are some cultural imbalances that exist when tr
 anslating from minor languages and/or niche subcultures into the dominant 
 language and/or literary market? Additionally\, what practical considerati
 ons do translators have when obtaining rights of the source text and negot
 iating control over their translations? What institutional and legal barri
 ers must one navigate? Are there creative loopholes to work around copyrig
 ht and other regulations regarding translation?  Finally\, we want to offe
 r a space for imagination. What would an ideal version of literary copyrig
 ht look like?OUR SPEAKERS:Esther Allen      Esther Allen is a professor in
  the Ph.D. Programs in French\, Comparative Literature\, and Latin America
 \, Iberian and Latino Cultures (LAILaC) at the City University of New York
  Graduate Center. Her translation of the Trilogy of Expectation by Antonio
  Di Benedetto (Zama\, The Silentiary\, and The Suicides) was supported by 
 a grant from the Guggenheim Foundation and Zama  was awarded the National 
 Translation Award by ALTA. She has been a Fellow at the Center for Scholar
 s and Writers at the New York Public Library and was named a Chevalier de 
 l'Ordre des arts et des lettres by the French government. Her essays and t
 ranslations have appeared in The New York Review of Books\, The LA Review 
 of Books\, Words Without Borders\, The Paris Review\, The Poetry Foundatio
 n\, Nimrod\, and other publications. She is currently completing a biograp
 hy of José Martí.&#160\;    Stine An Stine An is a poet\, translator\, a
 nd performer based in New York City. She holds a BA in Literature from Har
 vard and an MFA in Literary Arts from Brown\, and has received fellowships
  and grants from The Poetry Project\, the PEN/Heim Translation Fund Grant\
 , Yaddo\, ALTA\, and the National Endowment for the Arts. Her publications
  include Today's Morning Vocabulary by Yoo Heekyung (2025)\, Winter Night 
 Rabbit Worries by Yoo Heekyung (2026)\, and S_MMER CR_SH (2025). Their deb
 ut poetry collection\, B-Dragon Suite (2026)\, won the 2023 Nightboat Poet
 ry Prize and is available from Nightboat Books. You can find her online at
  gregorspamsa.com (https://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=htt
 p:%2F%2Fgregorspamsa.com%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmcalver%40iu.edu%7Cda9fe28
 3257c4b68c88608df2a1b8107%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639
 275969750963194%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLj
 AuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&am
 p\;sdata=i8K%2FmFJo4j08Q8t%2BK%2FfKjEAOu6h0IGLALe4StxZ%2F5B0%3D&amp\;reser
 ved=0).Soleil DavidSoleil Davíd is a poet and translator from the Philipp
 ines. She is a National Endowment for the Arts fellow\, a winner of the Do
 n Carlos Palanca Memorial Awards for Literature\, and has received support
  from both the Bread Loaf Writers' and Translators' Conferences\, MacDowel
 l\, and the DC Commission on the Arts and Humanities\, among others. Her t
 ranslation of Carlo Paulo Pacolor's short story collection The Compendium 
 of Impossible Objects is forthcoming from Tilted Axis Press.&#160\;Rachel 
 CordascoRachel Cordasco founded the website SFinTranslation.com in 2016 an
 d regularly reviews speculative fiction in translation for Strange Horizon
 s and other magazines. Her translations from the Italian of Clelia Farris'
 s science fiction have been published in Clarkesworld Magazine\, Future Sc
 ience Fiction Digest\, and elsewhere. Rachel's books on SFT are Out of Thi
 s World: Speculative Fiction in Translation From the Cold War to the New M
 illennium (University of Illinois Press) and Speculative Fiction in Transl
 ation From Around the World (University of Wales Press).Mordecai MartinMor
 decai Martin is a 5th generation New Yorker\, a writer\, and a translator 
 of Yiddish and Hebrew poetry\, prose\, and scripture. He is a 2026-2027 Tr
 anslation Fellow at the Yiddish Book Center in Amherst\, MA and holds an M
 FA from Randolph College. His translation work can be seen in literary jou
 rnals such as Exchanges\, Turkoslavia\, ANMLY\, and Asymptote. He lives in
  Mexico City with his wife\, child and Pharaoh-Let-My People-Go the cat.  
 &#160\;SPEAKERS' PRECIRCULATED STATEMENTS:Esther Allen        It's startli
 ng to be asked the same question in the same week by Indiana University an
 d the Anthropic Copyright Settlement Administrator. My answers to the Admi
 nistrator varied considerably\, depending on the translated book in questi
 on. Context is everything\, and for this IU symposium\, the question becom
 es a different one\, not "Who Owns (Your) Translation?" but "Who Owns Tran
 slation (Itself— as creative practice\, cause\, academic and publishing 
 field)?"              "Own" can be synonymous with "appropriate\," "posses
 s\," "control\," and "enslave." But it's a capacious verb\, and can also o
 perate in a different register to mean "admit\," "acknowledge\," "feel res
 ponsible for\," or even "value" or "rejoice in." The first sense\, associa
 ted with the conventional paradigm of fidelity\, positions translation as 
 a product sold in the information marketplace\, owned by whoever has the m
 oney and power to control it.              The second register of "own" br
 ings to mind the counter-paradigm of intimacy recently proposed by Sophie 
 Collins. "While 'fidelity' implies the presence of a primary source of pow
 er\, 'intimacy' indicates a mutual\, consensual and willing exchange betwe
 en two or more subjects."1              An entire lifetime ago (my specifi
 c lifetime)\, Guy Debord warned of the "information theorists…\," "vassa
 ls of the lords of technocratic feudalism."2 Yet he maintained that words 
 are "fundamentally alien" to power\; language "contain(s) forces that can 
 upset the most careful calculations." Poetry\, he concluded\, "thus depend
 s on the richest possibilities for living."              Nobody can own tr
 anslation. Subjectivities\, in intimate exchange with others\, can take re
 sponsibility for saving humanity and its languages from the information ma
 chines.      Stine An        "Toward a Cornucopia of Copies"              
 Notes: The structure of this essay is copied from Julia Sanches's statemen
 t for the 2024 Heim Colloqium "They're Not Children": Imagined Readers in 
 Contemporary Literary Translation.              1. All writing is derivati
 ve. Everything is a copy of a copy. Even the alphabet is a copy of an ox\,
  a house\, a throwing stick. I\, too\, am a copy.              2. Before t
 he advent of printing technology in certain jurisdictions\, copies were cr
 eated by hand\, letter-by-letter\, literally\, by highly-trained\, literat
 e individuals\, sometimes free and often not. To copy was to write.       
        3. As a noun\, copy comes from the Latin copia ("an abundance\, amp
 le supply\, plenty"). From com ("with") plus ops ("power\, wealth\, resour
 ces"). A copy\, then\, makes possible a copious opulence.              4. 
 As a verb\, copy cops from the Medieval Latin copiare ("to transcribe"). T
 o copy was "to write an original text many times." To copy is to multiply 
 originals. Enter\, translator.              5. According to the Copyright 
 Alliance\, copyright provides property rights to encourage authors "to cre
 ate new works and to make those works available to the public to enjoy." C
 opyright supports innovation.              6. Keaton\, Bale\, Clooney\, Af
 fleck\, Pattinson—each Batman brings their own interpretation. Each perf
 ormance is derivative and its own original thing. Once Jeremy Tiang pointe
 d this out\, I see translators as actors.              7. The translator i
 s the author of a translation. Translators increase\, grow\, and multiply 
 the possibilities of the text. Translators are actors of texts\, but are t
 hey granted an author-actor's authority?              8. Translators do a 
 lot more than act. Translators promote\, collaborate\, strategize\, advoca
 te\, move\, profess\, and wrangle to introduce texts into a new language a
 nd market. Translators remain undercompensated and undercredited.         
      9. Copyright\, when employed well\, increases abundance. More abundan
 ce for the translator in credit and compensation. More abundance for the p
 ublic and literary culture. Copyright\, when employed wrong\, protects hoa
 rded scarcity.              10. Can you imagine not giving an actor credit
  for their work? Yet\, some publishers still deny translators their copyri
 ght and refuse to put translator names on the front cover. Why?      Solei
 l David        The idealist in me wants to say that a work of literature i
 n translation belongs to all readers of the source and target languages. A
 fter all\, all ideas\, words\, and turns of phrases do not come from the v
 oid\, but rather through writers' continued presence in the world\, that i
 s\, in their relations with other human beings. But that idealism is quick
 ly shattered by the fact that a translator has to eat. So\, too\, does a w
 riter. And the business of publishing all over the world rarely allows a w
 riter nor translator to eat. In some cases\, usually in the Global North\,
  an author is paid handsomely\, or at the very least fairly\, for the labo
 r of writing\, and is again paid licensing fees for the use of their work 
 as the source of translations.              A translator is usually paid o
 nce. There are royalties\, maybe.              In my career of underpaid\,
  thankless jobs\, being a translator is yet another iteration. But strange
 ly enough\, my translation pays more than my poetry\, but that's for anoth
 er colloquium. Also\, very importantly\, I translate from a language in th
 e Global South\, where the currency and economy are depressed more than pa
 rtly because of the United States and the Global North's international and
  monetary policies. I have the dubious distinction of being a translator w
 ho will most probably get paid more\, in terms of purchasing power\, than 
 my author.              Questions of history\, comparative standards of li
 ving\, and labor come up when I wrestle with this issue. Painful glimpses 
 of the long-simmering tensions between those of the homeland and those of 
 the diaspora make themselves visible. What\, then\, is our responsibility 
 towards our authors\, as translators trying to make a living in better-fun
 ded countries?      Rachel CordascoIn thinking about "who owns translation
 s\," I'm reminded of Robin Myers's remark in an interview for the Booker P
 rizes website that "[t]he practice of reading\, writing\, and publishing t
 ranslations reminds us\, and humbles us all in doing so\, that every book 
 is a collaborative and continually (re)generative creation\, expansive and
  refractive across time and space." A published translation can't happen w
 ithout the author and the original text\, the translator and the translate
 d text\, the editor\, and the publisher. Thinking about "ownership" concep
 tually when it comes to translation\, though\, shifts the discussion. Afte
 r all\, without the translator\, the translation wouldn't exist\, and the 
 author would be left without their text being read by a wider audience\, a
 nd the editor and publisher wouldn't have a book to work with. In this way
 \, I tend to see the translator as the "owner" of the translation because 
 it is their creation. Ultimately\, the translator must take "ownership" of
  their work in terms of standing behind the choices they've made\, but onc
 e it's out in the world\, the text becomes a different kind of creature. Y
 es\, the translator remains the translator\, but I like to think of this n
 ew relationship with readers as one of "creating" and "bestowing." A trans
 lation\, once it is in the world\, is consumed by the author of the origin
 al text\, the editor\, and readers\, and takes on a new life as a text in 
 conversation with other texts. The translator has brought it into another 
 language\, and their name should be clearly associated with that translati
 on (on the cover\, on the title page\, in marketing copy\, etc.) because i
 t is their work. How readers will then accept it\, review it\, and discuss
  it\, though\, is nothing that the translator can control. So I suppose th
 at this discussion could veer into issues of "control\," as well.Mordecai 
 Martin        On a flight between London and New York\, halfway over the A
 tlantic\, a man gets up from his seat\, goes to the bathroom and emerges a
  terrifying visage. In a perfect replica of an SS officer's uniform\, righ
 t down to the totenkopf insignia\, he wields an assault rifle he has someh
 ow smuggled onto the plane.        "Alright\," he snarls\, "who's a Jew?!"
               "ווייסו ואָס?"              Says the black hatted H
 asid in the exit row to his similarly dressed companion\,              "ס
 ׳איז אַ גוטע פֿראַגע!"              "Y'know something? It's 
 a good question!"        Does the joke change if the flight is from Brusse
 ls? Vilnius? Warsaw? Kyiv? St. Petersburg? Tel Aviv?        Yiddish has sp
 ent its modern history as a language easier to locate in certain municipal
 ities than inside contested borders. Jewish identity remains almost as con
 tested throughout its history as those borders. Those debates have always 
 been religious\, political\, and deeply complicated. Thus the above joke. 
 Did you get the joke? If so\, do you own the joke?        As a translator 
 of Ukrainian-born immigrant New Yorker Yiddish cartoonist\, humorist\, pup
 peteer\, communist Yosl Cutler\, I have been constantly confronted with wh
 o owns his works. From hunts for heirs and copyright holders\, to glossing
  Cutler's politics and my own\, to debating whether or not finding informa
 tion in public Yiddish archives held by Israel violates a commitment to th
 e Palestinian Acadaemic and Cultural Boycott of Israel\, my project is rif
 e with questions of what is Jewish humor and who gets to say that it is th
 eirs/ours/yours/mine.        Cutler's book is called מונטערגאַנג
 \, a punning title that plays on the words for cheerful and disaster. I re
 nder it as MERRILY WE GO A-WRONG. Merrily go a-wrong with us?
GEO:39.165496;-86.519719
LAST-MODIFIED:20261103T144828Z
LOCATION:MORRISON HALL
