 nslations-on-literary-ri
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">T
 he Heim Colloquium is made possible by a generous donation to Indiana Univ
 ersity by the family of Michael Henry Heim (1943 – 2012)\, one of the mo
 st eminent literary translators of the late twentieth- and early twenty-fi
 rst century in the US.</span> The Heim bequest allows Indiana University t
 o organize events on themes relevant to the many facets of Michael Henry H
 eim's work. For our inaugural colloquia\, we chose to focus on the imagine
 d reader of literary translations (in 2024) and translator paratexts\, the
  introductions and afterwords translators have written about their work (i
 n 2025). For this year's event\, we invited a core group of translation-fo
 cused graduate students at IU Bloomington to take the lead in forming the 
 questions to be addressed and inviting a panel of experts to help us addre
 ss them.</p><p>  The title of this year's colloquium is "Who Owns Translat
 ion?"</p><p>  Who owns translations? Is it the translator? The author of t
 he source text? The publisher? The "foundation" and other estates that con
 trol the rights? Our colloquium explores the question of ownership from th
 eoretical\, practical\, and speculative perspectives. First\, what are som
 e ways that the translator conceptually "owns" the translation? At issue a
 re questions of how translators make choices that redistribute narrative a
 uthority\, how they develop a responsibility for the work of another\, and
  whether certain texts "invite" co-ownership while others do not.</p><p>  
 We would also like to explore how the literary market shapes questions of 
 ownership. How does the contemporary culture of self-promotion and social 
 media play a role in this ownership? What are some cultural imbalances tha
 t exist when translating from minor languages and/or niche subcultures int
 o the dominant language and/or literary market? Additionally\, what practi
 cal considerations do translators have when obtaining rights of the source
  text and negotiating control over their translations? What institutional 
 and legal barriers must one navigate? Are there creative loopholes to work
  around copyright and other regulations regarding translation?</p><p>  Fin
 ally\, we want to offer a space for imagination. What would an ideal versi
 on of literary copyright look like?</p><p><strong>OUR SPEAKERS:</strong></
 p><div class="lw_accordion"><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class=
 "lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">Est
 her Allen</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden
 ="true">      Esther Allen is a professor in the Ph.D. Programs in French\
 , Comparative Literature\, and Latin America\, Iberian and Latino Cultures
  (LAILaC) at the City University of New York Graduate Center. Her translat
 ion of the Trilogy of Expectation by Antonio Di Benedetto (<i>Zama\, The S
 ilentiary\, </i>and <i>The Suicides</i>) was supported by a grant from the
  Guggenheim Foundation and <i>Zama </i> was awarded the National Translati
 on Award by ALTA. She has been a Fellow at the Center for Scholars and Wri
 ters at the New York Public Library and was named a Chevalier de l'Ordre d
 es arts et des lettres by the French government. Her essays and translatio
 ns have appeared in <i>The New York Review of Books\, The LA Review of Boo
 ks\, Words Without Borders\, The Paris Review\, The Poetry Foundation\, Ni
 mrod\, </i>and other publications. She is currently completing a biography
  of José Martí.&#160\;    </div></section><section class="lw_accordion_b
 lock"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" t
 ype="button">Stine An</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content"
  aria-hidden="true"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><picture clas
 s="lw_image lw_image33171  lw_align_left lw_column_width_half"><source typ
