 %7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=i8K%2FmFJo4j08Q8t%2BK%2FfKjEAOu6h0IGLALe4StxZ%2F5B0%3
 D&amp\;reserved=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" data-auth="No
 tApplicable" data-linkindex="0" data-ogsc="">gregorspamsa.com</a>.</span><
 /div></section><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion
 _block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">Soleil David</bu
 tton></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="true"><span
  data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><picture class="lw_image lw_image33227
   lw_align_left lw_column_width_half"><source type="image/webp" srcset="ht
 tps://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/429/width/500/height/432/33227
 _Soleil_David_headshot.rev.1792700211.webp 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live
 /image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/height/432/33227_Soleil_David_headshot.r
 ev.1792700211.webp 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg"
  srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/429/width/500/heigh
 t/432/33227_Soleil_David_headshot.rev.1792700211.jpg 2x\, https://events.i
 u.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/height/432/33227_Soleil_David_
 headshot.rev.1792700211.jpg 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="500"
  height="432" alt="Portrait of woman outside" data-alt="Portrait of woman 
 outside" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/429/width/500/height/43
 2/33227_Soleil_David_headshot.rev.1792700211.jpg" title="Soleil David Head
 shot" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/429/width/500/
 height/432/33227_Soleil_David_headshot.rev.1792700211.jpg 2x\, https://eve
 nts.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/height/432/33227_Soleil_D
 avid_headshot.rev.1792700211.jpg 3x" data-max-w="2875" data-max-h="2486" l
 oading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture>Soleil Davíd is a poet and
  translator from the Philippines. She is a National Endowment for the Arts
  fellow\, a winner of the Don Carlos Palanca Memorial Awards for Literatur
 e\, and has received support from both the Bread Loaf Writers' and Transla
 tors' Conferences\, MacDowell\, and the DC Commission on the Arts and Huma
 nities\, among others. Her translation of Carlo Paulo Pacolor's short stor
 y collection <i>The Compendium of Impossible Objects</i> is forthcoming fr
 om Tilted Axis Press.&#160\;</span></div></section><section class="lw_acco
 rdion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="f
 alse" type="button">Rachel Cordasco</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_
 block_content" aria-hidden="true"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"
 ><picture class="lw_image lw_image33228  lw_align_left lw_column_width_hal
 f"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scal
 e/2x/gid/429/width/500/height/585/33228_Cordasco_headshot.rev.1792700256.w
 ebp 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/heigh
 t/585/33228_Cordasco_headshot.rev.1792700256.webp 3x" data-origin="respons
 ive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/s
 cale/2x/gid/429/width/500/height/585/33228_Cordasco_headshot.rev.179270025
 6.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/hei
 ght/585/33228_Cordasco_headshot.rev.1792700256.jpg 3x" data-origin="respon
 sive"/><img width="500" height="585" alt="Picture of woman" data-alt="Pict
 ure of woman" src="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/429/width/500/heig
 ht/585/33228_Cordasco_headshot.rev.1792700256.jpg" title="Rachel Cordasco 
 headshot" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/429/width/
 500/height/585/33228_Cordasco_headshot.rev.1792700256.jpg 2x\, https://eve
 nts.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/height/585/33228_Cordasco
 _headshot.rev.1792700256.jpg 3x" data-max-w="2734" data-max-h="3200" loadi
 ng="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture>Rachel Cordasco founded the web
 site SFinTranslation.com in 2016 and regularly reviews speculative fiction
  in translation for Strange Horizons and other magazines. Her translations
  from the Italian of Clelia Farris's science fiction have been published i
 n Clarkesworld Magazine\, Future Science Fiction Digest\, and elsewhere. R
 achel's books on SFT are Out of This World: Speculative Fiction in Transla
 tion From the Cold War to the New Millennium (University of Illinois Press
 ) and Speculative Fiction in Translation From Around the World (University
  of Wales Press).</span></div></section><section class="lw_accordion_block
 "><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type=
 "button">Mordecai Martin</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_conte
 nt" aria-hidden="true"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><picture c
 lass="lw_image lw_image33229  lw_align_left lw_column_width_half"><source 
 type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/42
 9/width/500/height/780/33229_Mordecai.rev.1792700284.webp 2x\, https://eve
 nts.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/height/780/33229_Mordecai
 .rev.1792700284.webp 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><source type="image/jpe
 g" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/429/width/500/hei
 ght/780/33229_Mordecai.rev.1792700284.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/
 image/scale/3x/gid/429/width/500/height/780/33229_Mordecai.rev.1792700284.
 jpg 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="500" height="780" alt="Pictu
 re of man\, hand on cheek" data-alt="Picture of man\, hand on cheek" src="
 https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/429/width/500/height/780/33229_Mordec
 ai.rev.1792700284.jpg" title="Mordecai&#xA0\;Martin Headshot" srcset="http
 s://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/429/width/500/height/780/33229_M
 ordecai.rev.1792700284.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/
 gid/429/width/500/height/780/33229_Mordecai.rev.1792700284.jpg 3x" data-ma
 x-w="2051" data-max-h="3199" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></pictu
 re>Mordecai Martin is a 5th generation New Yorker\, a writer\, and a trans
 lator of Yiddish and Hebrew poetry\, prose\, and scripture. He is a 2026-2
 027 Translation Fellow at the Yiddish Book Center in Amherst\, MA and hold
 s an MFA from Randolph College. His translation work can be seen in litera
 ry journals such as Exchanges\, Turkoslavia\, ANMLY\, and Asymptote. He li
 ves in Mexico City with his wife\, child and Pharaoh-Let-My People-Go the 
 cat.</span><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><br /></span></div></s
 ection></div><p>  &#160\;</p><p><strong>SPEAKERS' PRECIRCULATED STATEMENTS
 :</strong></p><div class="lw_accordion"><section class="lw_accordion_block
 "><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type=
 "button">Esther Allen</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content"
  aria-hidden="true"><p>        It's startling to be asked the same questio
 n in the same week by Indiana University and the Anthropic Copyright Settl
 ement Administrator. My answers to the Administrator varied considerably\,
  depending on the translated book in question. Context is everything\, and
  for this IU symposium\, the question becomes a different one\, not "Who O
 wns (Your) Translation?" but "Who Owns Translation (Itself— as creative 
 practice\, cause\, academic and publishing field)?"      </p><p>        "O
 wn" can be synonymous with "appropriate\," "possess\," "control\," and "en
 slave." But it's a capacious verb\, and can also operate in a different re
 gister to mean "admit\," "acknowledge\," "feel responsible for\," or even 
 "value" or "rejoice in." The first sense\, associated with the conventiona
 l paradigm of fidelity\, positions translation as a product sold in the in
 formation marketplace\, owned by whoever has the money and power to contro
 l it.      </p><p>        The second register of "own" brings to mind the 
 counter-paradigm of intimacy recently proposed by Sophie Collins. "While '
 fidelity' implies the presence of a primary source of power\, 'intimacy' i
 ndicates a mutual\, consensual and willing exchange between two or more su
 bjects."1      </p><p>        An entire lifetime ago (my specific lifetime
 )\, Guy Debord warned of the "information theorists…\," "vassals of the 
 lords of technocratic feudalism."2 Yet he maintained that words are "funda
 mentally alien" to power\; language "contain(s) forces that can upset the 
 most careful calculations." Poetry\, he concluded\, "thus depends on the r
 ichest possibilities for living."      </p><p>        Nobody can own trans
 lation. Subjectivities\, in intimate exchange with others\, can take respo
 nsibility for saving humanity and its languages from the information machi
 nes.      </p></div></section><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 clas
 s="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">S
 tine An</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="
 true"><p>        "Toward a Cornucopia of Copies"      </p><p>        Notes
 : The structure of this essay is copied from Julia Sanches's statement for
  the 2024 Heim Colloqium "They're Not Children": Imagined Readers in Conte
 mporary Literary Translation.      </p><p>        1. All writing is deriva
 tive. Everything is a copy of a copy. Even the alphabet is a copy of an ox
 \, a house\, a throwing stick. I\, too\, am a copy.      </p><p>        2.
  Before the advent of printing technology in certain jurisdictions\, copie
 s were created by hand\, letter-by-letter\, literally\, by highly-trained\
 , literate individuals\, sometimes free and often not. To copy was to writ
 e.      </p><p>        3. As a noun\, copy comes from the Latin copia ("an
  abundance\, ample supply\, plenty"). From com ("with") plus ops ("power\,
  wealth\, resources"). A copy\, then\, makes possible a copious opulence. 
      </p><p>        4. As a verb\, copy cops from the Medieval Latin copia
 re ("to transcribe"). To copy was "to write an original text many times." 
 To copy is to multiply originals. Enter\, translator.      </p><p>        
 5. According to the Copyright Alliance\, copyright provides property right
 s to encourage authors "to create new works and to make those works availa
 ble to the public to enjoy." Copyright supports innovation.      </p><p>  
       6. Keaton\, Bale\, Clooney\, Affleck\, Pattinson—each Batman bring
 s their own interpretation. Each performance is derivative and its own ori
 ginal thing. Once Jeremy Tiang pointed this out\, I see translators as act
 ors.      </p><p>        7. The translator is the author of a translation.
  Translators increase\, grow\, and multiply the possibilities of the text.
  Translators are actors of texts\, but are they granted an author-actor's 
 authority?      </p><p>        8. Translators do a lot more than act. Tran
 slators promote\, collaborate\, strategize\, advocate\, move\, profess\, a
 nd wrangle to introduce texts into a new language and market. Translators 
 remain undercompensated and undercredited.      </p><p>        9. Copyrigh
 t\, when employed well\, increases abundance. More abundance for the trans
 lator in credit and compensation. More abundance for the public and litera
 ry culture. Copyright\, when employed wrong\, protects hoarded scarcity.  
     </p><p>        10. Can you imagine not giving an actor credit for thei
 r work? Yet\, some publishers still deny translators their copyright and r
 efuse to put translator names on the front cover. Why?      </p></div></se
 ction><section class="lw_accordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_ti
 tle"><button aria-expanded="false" type="button">Soleil David</button></h4
 ><div class="lw_accordion_block_content" aria-hidden="true"><p>        The
  idealist in me wants to say that a work of literature in translation belo
 ngs to all readers of the source and target languages. After all\, all ide
 as\, words\, and turns of phrases do not come from the void\, but rather t
 hrough writers' continued presence in the world\, that is\, in their relat
 ions with other human beings. But that idealism is quickly shattered by th
 e fact that a translator has to eat. So\, too\, does a writer. And the bus
 iness of publishing all over the world rarely allows a writer nor translat
 or to eat. In some cases\, usually in the Global North\, an author is paid
  handsomely\, or at the very least fairly\, for the labor of writing\, and
  is again paid licensing fees for the use of their work as the source of t
 ranslations.      </p><p>        A translator is usually paid once. There 
 are royalties\, maybe.      </p><p>        In my career of underpaid\, tha
 nkless jobs\, being a translator is yet another iteration. But strangely e
 nough\, my translation pays more than my poetry\, but that's for another c
 olloquium. Also\, very importantly\, I translate from a language in the Gl
 obal South\, where the currency and economy are depressed more than partly
  because of the United States and the Global North's international and mon
 etary policies. I have the dubious distinction of being a translator who w
 ill most probably get paid more\, in terms of purchasing power\, than my a
 uthor.      </p><p>        Questions of history\, comparative standards of
  living\, and labor come up when I wrestle with this issue. Painful glimps
 es of the long-simmering tensions between those of the homeland and those 
 of the diaspora make themselves visible. What\, then\, is our responsibili
 ty towards our authors\, as translators trying to make a living in better-
 funded countries?      </p></div></section><section class="lw_accordion_bl
 ock"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded="false" ty
 pe="button">Rachel Cordasco</button></h4><div class="lw_accordion_block_co
 ntent" aria-hidden="true"><p><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">In t
 hinking about "who owns translations\," I'm reminded of Robin Myers's rema
 rk in an interview for the Booker Prizes website that "[t]he practice of r
 eading\, writing\, and publishing translations reminds us\, and humbles us
  all in doing so\, that every book is a collaborative and continually (re)
 generative creation\, expansive and refractive across time and space." A p
 ublished translation can't happen without the author and the original text
 \, the translator and the translated text\, the editor\, and the publisher
 . Thinking about "ownership" conceptually when it comes to translation\, t
 hough\, shifts the discussion. After all\, without the translator\, the tr
 anslation wouldn't exist\, and the author would be left without their text
  being read by a wider audience\, and the editor and publisher wouldn't ha
 ve a book to work with. In this way\, I tend to see the translator as the 
 "owner" of the translation because it is their creation. Ultimately\, the 
 translator must take "ownership" of their work in terms of standing behind
  the choices they've made\, but once it's out in the world\, the text beco
 mes a different kind of creature. Yes\, the translator remains the transla
 tor\, but I like to think of this new relationship with readers as one of 
 "creating" and "bestowing." A translation\, once it is in the world\, is c
 onsumed by the author of the original text\, the editor\, and readers\, an
 d takes on a new life as a text in conversation with other texts. The tran
 slator has brought it into another language\, and their name should be cle
 arly associated with that translation (on the cover\, on the title page\, 
 in marketing copy\, etc.) because it is their work. How readers will then 
 accept it\, review it\, and discuss it\, though\, is nothing that the tran
 slator can control. So I suppose that this discussion could veer into issu
 es of "control\," as well.</span></p></div></section><section class="lw_ac
 cordion_block"><h4 class="lw_accordion_block_title"><button aria-expanded=
 "false" type="button">Mordecai Martin</button></h4><div class="lw_accordio
 n_block_content" aria-hidden="true"><div data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody
 ">        On a flight between London and New York\, halfway over the Atlan
 tic\, a man gets up from his seat\, goes to the bathroom and emerges a ter
 rifying visage. In a perfect replica of an SS officer's uniform\, right do
 wn to the totenkopf insignia\, he wields an assault rifle he has somehow s
 muggled onto the plane.<br /><br />        "Alright\," he snarls\, "who's 
 a Jew?!"      </div><div style="padding-left: 360px\;">        "ווייס
 טו ואָס?"      </div><div>        Says the black hatted Hasid in the 
 exit row to his similarly dressed companion\,      </div><div style="paddi
 ng-left: 320px\;">        "ס׳איז אַ גוטע פֿראַגע!"      </d
 iv><div><br />        "Y'know something? It's a good question!"<br /><br /
 >        Does the joke change if the flight is from Brussels? Vilnius? War
 saw? Kyiv? St. Petersburg? Tel Aviv?<br /><br />        Yiddish has spent 
 its modern history as a language easier to locate in certain municipalitie
 s than inside contested borders. Jewish identity remains almost as contest
 ed throughout its history as those borders. Those debates have always been
  religious\, political\, and deeply complicated. Thus the above joke. Did 
 you get the joke? If so\, do you own the joke?<br /><br />        As a tra
 nslator of Ukrainian-born immigrant New Yorker Yiddish cartoonist\, humori
 st\, puppeteer\, communist Yosl Cutler\, I have been constantly confronted
  with who owns his works. From hunts for heirs and copyright holders\, to 
 glossing Cutler's politics and my own\, to debating whether or not finding
  information in public Yiddish archives held by Israel violates a commitme
 nt to the Palestinian Acadaemic and Cultural Boycott of Israel\, my projec
 t is rife with questions of what is Jewish humor and who gets to say that 
 it is theirs/ours/yours/mine.<br /><br /><br />        Cutler's book is ca
 lled מונטערגאַנג\, a punning title that plays on the words for c
 heerful and disaster. I render it as MERRILY WE GO A-WRONG. Merrily go a-w
 rong with us?      </div></div></section></div>
