ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/137/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,768,768/33268_vieira-estela2.rev.1793043290.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:The Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) we
 lcomes Dr. Estela Vieira for Machado de Assis and the Female Reader\, as p
 art of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.Tuesday\, November 1
 0th | 4–5PM | Hamilton Lugar School | GA2067Abstract: Machado de Assis a
 nd the Female Reader  Widely regarded as one of Brazil's greatest writers\
 , Joaquim Maria Machado de Assis (1839-1908) is known for directly address
 ing and implicating his readers\, but what happens when that reader is a w
 oman and that dialogue turns into a critical force in his fiction? This pr
 esentation will first briefly summarize my broader research on women reade
 rs\, writers\, and literary agency in Machado de Assis\, surveying women-l
 ed scholarship on Machado and the author's personal library and correspond
 ence to show his concern for women's active role in Brazilian literary cul
 ture. The core of the talk will focus on his penultimate novel\, Esaú e J
 acó (Esau and Jacob 1904)\, and analyze the ways in which the narrator an
 d female reader intersect and evolve in the novel. As she emerges as confi
 dante\, adversary\, and ironic double of the protagonist\, Flora\, she als
 o begins to usurp the narrator/writer. This development unravels into a th
 eorization of the relationship between literature and women (as readers\, 
 writers\, characters)\, a topic Machado was keen to engage with as he alig
 ns reading with feminine critical agency.&#160\;Bio:  Dr. Estela Vieira is
  a scholar of Lusophone literary and cultural studies with special interes
 ts in nineteenth-century literature\, film\, and women writers. Her first 
 book studied the function and representation of the interior space in thre
 e realist novels. She is currently working on a manuscript that studies fe
 male authorship in nineteenth-century Portugal. She is also interested in 
 film and has published articles on film and literature\, political memory 
 and cinema\, and Iberian women filmmakers. Her research has focused on nin
 eteenth-century writers including Machado de Assis\, Eça de Queirós\, J
 úlio Dinis\; on Portugal's historical memory and cultural identity\; and 
 on modernist poetics and contemporary authors such as José Saramago.
LAST-MODIFIED:20261106T160032Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2463760-clacs-faculty-research-sympo
 sium-with-estela
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p data-pm-slice="1 1 []">  The Center for La
 tin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS) welcomes <strong>Dr. Estela Vie
 ira</strong> for <strong><em>Machado de Assis and the Female Reader</em></
 strong>\, as part of the Fall 2026 CLACS Faculty Research Symposium.</p><p
  data-pm-slice="1 1 []"><strong>Tuesday\, November 10th | 4–5PM | Hamilt
 on Lugar School | GA2067</strong></p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong> Machado
  de Assis and the Female Reader<br />  Widely regarded as one of Brazil's 
 greatest writers\, Joaquim Maria Machado de Assis (1839-1908) is known for
  directly addressing and implicating his readers\, but what happens when t
 hat reader is a woman and that dialogue turns into a critical force in his
  fiction? This presentation will first briefly summarize my broader resear
 ch on women readers\, writers\, and literary agency in Machado de Assis\, 
 surveying women-led scholarship on Machado and the author's personal libra
 ry and correspondence to show his concern for women's active role in Brazi
 lian literary culture. The core of the talk will focus on his penultimate 
 novel\, Esaú e Jacó (Esau and Jacob 1904)\, and analyze the ways in whic
 h the narrator and female reader intersect and evolve in the novel. As she
  emerges as confidante\, adversary\, and ironic double of the protagonist\
 , Flora\, she also begins to usurp the narrator/writer. This development u
 nravels into a theorization of the relationship between literature and wom
 en (as readers\, writers\, characters)\, a topic Machado was keen to engag
 e with as he aligns reading with feminine critical agency.&#160\;</p><p><s
 trong>Bio:</strong></p><p>  Dr. Estela Vieira is a scholar of Lusophone li
 terary and cultural studies with special interests in nineteenth-century l
 iterature\, film\, and women writers. Her first book studied the function 
 and representation of the interior space in three realist novels. She is c
 urrently working on a manuscript that studies female authorship in ninetee
 nth-century Portugal. She is also interested in film and has published art
 icles on film and literature\, political memory and cinema\, and Iberian w
 omen filmmakers. Her research has focused on nineteenth-century writers in
 cluding Machado de Assis\, Eça de Queirós\, Júlio Dinis\; on Portugal's
  historical memory and cultural identity\; and on modernist poetics and co
 ntemporary authors such as José Saramago.</p><p><picture class="lw_image 
 lw_image33455  lw_align_left"><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="ima
 ge/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/137/width/5
 00/height/669/33455_clacs_symposium_-_estela_vieira.rev.1793980828.webp 2x
 " data-origin="responsive"/><source media="(max-width: 500px)" type="image
 /jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/137/width/500
 /height/669/33455_clacs_symposium_-_estela_vieira.rev.1793980828.jpg 2x" d
 ata-origin="responsive"/><source media="(min-width: 501px)" type="image/we
 bp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/137/width/609/height/815/
