X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_11
SUMMARY:Art & a Movie at the Eskenazi Museum: Unfinished Business
DTSTART:20261115T200000Z
DTEND:20261115T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20261020T174717Z
UID:20261115T200000Z-2458125@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261115T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/234/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1200,1800/1928_julie.rev.1569359010.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Functions, festivals, and celebratio
 ns,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,Performances, shows, and fil
 ms
DESCRIPTION:Steven Soderbergh's film\, The Christophers\, raises fascinati
 ng questions about authorship\, authenticity\, and value. We prize origina
 lity\, art as the brainchild of a single artist\, completed in a burst of 
 passionate activity. However\, many art objects defy this assumption: they
  may be products of workshops\; completed in stages or left incomplete\; a
 nd/or repaired once or multiple times. Join Dr. Graves to explore how obje
 cts in the museum's collections have been affected by conservators\, resto
 rers\, assistants\, and the original artists\, themselves. We'll tour the 
 galleries\, examining items ranging from antiquity to only a few decades o
 ld. Meet at the museum's 7th St. entrance.  Bio: Jean Graves is the Meier 
 Chair of Education at the Eskenazi Museum of Art. She earned her MA in Art
  History from Boston University and PhD in Curriculum and Instruction from
  Indiana University.
GEO:39.169144;-86.519739
LAST-MODIFIED:20261020T214717Z
LOCATION:SIDNEY AND LOIS ESKENAZI MUSEUM OF ART
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2457787-art-a-movie-at-the-eskenazi-
 museum-unfinished-busine
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p style="font-weight: 400\;">  Steven Soderb
 ergh's film\, <em>The Christophers</em>\, raises fascinating questions abo
 ut authorship\, authenticity\, and value. We prize originality\, art as th
 e brainchild of a single artist\, completed in a burst of passionate activ
 ity. However\, many art objects defy this assumption: they may be products
  of workshops\; completed in stages or left incomplete\; and/or repaired o
 nce or multiple times. Join Dr. Graves to explore how objects in the museu
 m's collections have been affected by conservators\, restorers\, assistant
 s\, and the original artists\, themselves. We'll tour the galleries\, exam
 ining items ranging from antiquity to only a few decades old. Meet at the 
 museum's 7<sup>th</sup> St. entrance.</p><p style="font-weight: 400\;">  B
 io: Jean Graves is the Meier Chair of Education at the Eskenazi Museum of 
 Art. She earned her MA in Art History from Boston University and PhD in Cu
 rriculum and Instruction from Indiana University.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<span class="spanWithBackgroundColor"><strong>Jea
 n A. Graves\, PhD</strong></span>\n<div>\n  Patricia and Joel Meier Chair 
 of Education\n</div>\n<div>\n  Sidney and Lois Eskenazi Museum of Art<br /
 >\n  gravesje@iu.edu\n</div>\n<div>\n  \n</div>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2458125
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/234/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1200\,1800/1928_julie.rev.1569359010.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Painting conservator\, Julie Ribits\, looks at f
 resco painting on easel.
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Following the I<a href="https://cinema.indiana.
 edu/upcoming-films/screening/2026-fall-sunday-november-15-100pm" target="_
 blank" rel="noopener">U Cinema screening of <em>The Christophers</em></a> 
 continue the conversation at the Eskenazi Museum of Art with a special pro
 gram led by Jean Graves\, Meier Chair of Education.\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:Architecture + Design, Eskenazi Museum of Art, IU Cinema, IU Eskenazi School of Art
END:VEVENT
