 Lugar School GA3067</p><div class="x_elementToProof"><strong>Abstract:</st
 rong> Can rules governing land and forests make people safer on Brazil's A
 mazon frontier? This talk brings together three studies of rural violence\
 , land registration\, and forest enforcement. Drawing on 24 years of recor
 ds from Brazil's Pastoral Land Commission\, I show that Indigenous people 
 account for a growing share of documented murder victims and examine how t
 he geography of recorded violence has shifted. I then assess two policies:
  Pará's land registration mandate\, which was followed by fewer recorded 
 threats and conflicts\, and Brazil's priority municipality program\, which
  substantially reduced deforestation. The evidence for the program's socia
 l benefits is less certain because estimates of violence depend on which m
 unicipalities are used for comparison. Together\, the studies show why eva
 luating environmental policy requires careful attention to both the limits
  of violence records and the reasons places were selected for intervention
