SUMMARY:You (probably) Haven't Seen This Before - Best Practices: Films fo
 r Safe Living
DTSTART:20261203T223000Z
DTEND:20261203T233000Z
DTSTAMP:20260902T132606Z
UID:20261203T223000Z-2405055@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261203T223000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,800,800/32331_Dec2026Rodriguez-headshot.rev.1788370251.pn
 g
CATEGORIES:Performances, shows, and films
DESCRIPTION:Dive deep into the archival collections of IU Libraries throug
 h a screening series of rare and unique archival films that center traditi
 onally underrepresented narratives and underrepresented motion-picture pra
 ctices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum of what is considered "cine
 ma."  In an effort to keep audiences open-minded and with limited pre-conc
 eptions of these rare works of celluloid\, descriptions of films to be sho
 wn will not be shared prior to screenings. Join us based on announced them
 es and the expectation that the works you see will fall outside of Hollywo
 od tropes\, outside of mainstream commercial expectations\, and outside of
  conventional filmmaking practices. Trust us\, you (probably) haven't seen
  these films before!December Program: Best Practices: Films for Safe Livin
 g curated by Dave RodriguezProgram Description: Can a film save your life?
  Can it give you a design for living? These will certainly try.Curator Bio
 : Dave Rodriguez is a media artist\, musician\, and curator from Miami\, F
 L\, currently based in Bloomington\, IN. His film and video work has scree
 ned in festivals and galleries across the Americas and Europe including th
 e Ann Arbor Film Festival\, the Chicago Underground Film Festival\, Mono N
 o Aware\, Third Horizon\, and others. He has over 15 years of professional
  experience working in film labs\, libraries\, archives\, and museum colle
 ctions\, publishing in places like Found Footage Magazine\, the Journal of
  Academic Librarianship\, and most recently in the Review of Japanese Cult
 ure and Society. In Bloomington he spends his time taking care of many pla
 nts\, one old cat\, and supporting local organizations like Cicada Cinema 
 and the Bloomington Synth Community.Please register below! Advance registr
 ation is strongly encouraged and deeply appreciated!
GEO:39.171006;-86.5168
LAST-MODIFIED:20260902T174122Z
LOCATION:Wells Library Screening Room 048
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2405055-you-probably-havent-seen
 -this-before-best-practices-
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Dive deep into the archival collections 
 of IU Libraries through a screening series of rare and unique archival fil
 ms that center traditionally underrepresented narratives and underrepresen
 ted motion-picture practices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum of wh
 at is considered "cinema."</p><p>  In an effort to keep audiences open-min
 ded and with limited pre-conceptions of these rare works of celluloid\, de
 scriptions of films to be shown will not be shared prior to screenings. Jo
 in us based on announced themes and the expectation that the works you see
  will fall outside of Hollywood tropes\, outside of mainstream commercial 
 expectations\, and outside of conventional filmmaking practices. Trust us\
 , you (probably) haven't seen these films before!</p><p><strong>December P
 rogram:</strong> Best Practices: Films for Safe Living curated by Dave Rod
 riguez</p><p><strong>Program Description:</strong> Can a film save your li
 fe? Can it give you a design for living? These will certainly try.</p><p><
 strong>Curator Bio:</strong> Dave Rodriguez is a media artist\, musician\,
  and curator from Miami\, FL\, currently based in Bloomington\, IN. His fi
 lm and video work has screened in festivals and galleries across the Ameri
 cas and Europe including the Ann Arbor Film Festival\, the Chicago Undergr
 ound Film Festival\, Mono No Aware\, Third Horizon\, and others. He has ov
 er 15 years of professional experience working in film labs\, libraries\, 
 archives\, and museum collections\, publishing in places like <i>Found Foo
 tage Magazine</i>\, the<i> Journal of Academic Librarianship</i>\, and mos
 t recently in the <i>Review of Japanese Culture and Society</i>. In Bloomi
 ngton he spends his time taking care of many plants\, one old cat\, and su
 pporting local organizations like Cicada Cinema and the Bloomington Synth 
 Community.</p><p><strong>Please register below! Advance registration is st
 rongly encouraged and deeply appreciated!</strong></p><p><br /><br /></p>
