BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2457690-iks-colloquium-junyoung-veronica-kim"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:IKS Colloquium - Junyoung Verónica Kim
DTSTART:20270129T170000Z
DTEND:20270129T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20261021T161957Z
UID:20270129T170000Z-2459412@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20270129T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/177/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,848,1020/33159_JVK_Fall_2025_.rev.1792504263.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:IKS Spring 2027 Colloquium Series  Junyoung Verónica Kim  Dep
 artment of Asian and Asian American Studies  Binghamton University  Gwangj
 u from Buenos Aires: Missing Bodies and Forgotten Geographies in Im Heung-
 soon's Good Light\, Good Air&#160\;Friday\, January 2912:00-1:00pmGISB 206
 7 | 355 N Eagleson Ave  On April 30th\, 1977\, fourteen mothers sent a let
 ter asking the military dictator Jorge Rafael Videla for the whereabouts o
 f their disappeared children and began to gather in silent protest in the 
 Plaza de Mayo\, Buenos Aires. Known as the Mothers of Plaza de Mayo\, th
 ey spurred a mass movement that continues today. Around the same time\, on
  May 18th\, 1980\, citizens of Gwangju\, South Korea\, launched a mass res
 istance movement against the dictatorship of Chun Doo-hwan. The South Kore
 an military retaliated in full force resulting in the massacre and disappe
 arance of thousands of Gwangju citizens. The connections and intimacies be
 tween the overlapping histories of these two cities demonstrate the ways i
 n which Global South countries were violently conscripted into dirty wars 
 against communism. Despite the explicit linkages\, why are the histories o
 f these two places (cities and nations) studied separately––divided by
  discrete disciplinary boundaries? How does insurgent memory both disrupt 
 dominant historical national narratives and yoke together these two geogra
 phies/histories/futurities? Moreover\, how does the present absence of the
  missing in Gwangju and the desaparecidos (disappeared) in Buenos Aire
 s haunt the urban geographies that are shaped through the ordinary violenc
 e of gentrification and urban development? Through a reading of Im Heung-s
 oon's video installation and documentary Good Light\, Good Air (2018\, 202
 0)\, Junyoung Verónica Kim explores the multiple vectors and scales of tr
 anspacific connectivity. Examined through Eyal Weizman's conceptualization
  of forensic architecture\, the ruins\, holes\, and missing parts of both 
 the material (bodies and urban geographies) and the epistemic (archives\, 
 knowledge production\, disciplinary formation) testify to what cannot be v
 isiblized or seen otherwise. In situating the Gwangju Uprising and the Arg
 entine dirty war as part and parcel of the diasporic\, this talk proposes 
 that paying attention to Korean-Latin American intimacies offers a possibi
 lity of fabulating a decolonial and demilitarized world.&#160\;  Open to t
 he public. A light lunch will be served.
GEO:39.170011;-86.517418
LAST-MODIFIED:20261021T201957Z
LOCATION:355 North Eagleson Avenue
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2457690-iks-colloquium-junyoung-vero
 nica-kim
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;">  IKS Spring
  2027 Colloquium Series</h5><h6 style="text-align: center\;"><br />  Junyo
 ung Verónica Kim</h6><p style="text-align: center\;">  Department of Asia
 n and Asian American Studies<br />  Binghamton University</p><p style="tex
 t-align: center\;">  Gwangju from Buenos Aires: Missing Bodies and Forgott
 en Geographies in Im Heung-soon's <em>Good Light\, Good Air&#160\;</em></p
 ><p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Friday\, January 29</strong></p><
 p style="text-align: center\;"><strong>12:00-1:00pm</strong></p><p style="
 text-align: center\;"><span class="a_GcMg font-feature-liga-off font-featu
 re-clig-off font-feature-calt-off text-decoration-none text-strikethrough-
 none"><strong>GISB 2067 | 355 N Eagleson Ave</strong><br /><br />  On Apri
 l 30th\, 1977\, fourteen mothers sent a letter asking the military dictato
 r Jorge Rafael Videla for the whereabouts of their disappeared children an
 d began to gather in silent protest in the Plaza de Mayo\, Buenos Aires.
  Known as the Mothers of Plaza de Mayo\, they spurred a mass movement th
 at continues today. Around the same time\, on May 18th\, 1980\, citizens o
 f Gwangju\, South Korea\, launched a mass resistance movement against the 
 dictatorship of Chun Doo-hwan. The South Korean military retaliated in ful
 l force resulting in the massacre and disappearance of thousands of Gwangj
 u citizens. The connections and intimacies between the overlapping histori
 es of these two cities demonstrate the ways in which Global South countrie
 s were violently conscripted into dirty wars against communism. Despite th
 e explicit linkages\, why are the histories of these two places (cities an
 d nations) studied separately––divided by discrete disciplinary bounda
 ries? How does insurgent memory both disrupt dominant historical national 
 narratives and yoke together these two geographies/histories/futurities? M
 oreover\, how does the present absence of the missing in Gwangju and the
  desaparecidos (disappeared) in Buenos Aires haunt the urban geographi
 es that are shaped through the ordinary violence of gentrification and urb
 an development? Through a reading of Im Heung-soon's video installation an
 d documentary Good Light\, Good Air (2018\, 2020)\, Junyoung Verónica Kim
  explores the multiple vectors and scales of transpacific connectivity. Ex
 amined through Eyal Weizman's conceptualization of forensic architecture\,
  the ruins\, holes\, and missing parts of both the material (bodies and ur
 ban geographies) and the epistemic (archives\, knowledge production\, disc
 iplinary formation) testify to what cannot be visiblized or seen otherwise
 . In situating the Gwangju Uprising and the Argentine dirty war as part an
 d parcel of the diasporic\, this talk proposes that paying attention to Ko
 rean-Latin American intimacies offers a possibility of fabulating a decolo
 nial and demilitarized world.&#160\;<br /><br />  Open to the public. A li
 ght lunch will be served.</span></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>iks@iu.edu</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2459412
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/177/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,848\,1020/33159_JVK_Fall_2025_.rev.179250426
 3.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>Please join us for a talk in the IKS Spring 2027 Co
 lloquium Series by Junyoung Verónica Kim\, titled "Gwangju from Buenos Ai
 res: Missing Bodies and Forgotten Geographies in Im Heung-soon's <em>Good 
 Light\, Good Air</em> "</p>
